===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q684}}The direction of the earth's [[rotation]], specifically 90°.
#: {{alti|en|E|q=abbreviation}}
#: {{ux|en|Portsmouth is to the '''east''' of Southampton.}}
#: {{ux|en|We live in the '''east''' of the country.}}
#: {{ux|en|The sun rises in the '''east'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The levanter wind is in/from the '''east'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1895|author=w:Thomas Hardy|title=Jude the Obscure
|passage=In a few hours the birds come to it from all points of the compass &ndash; '''east''', west, north, and south {{...}}}}
# The eastern region or area; the inhabitants thereof. {{defdate|circa 1300}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1855|author=John Reynolds|title=My Own Times: Embracing Also the History of My Life|page=271
|passage=We, in the west, agreed amongst ourselves that a penitentiary should be erected with our half of the money arising as above stated; and the '''east''' agreed to improve the country in their vicinity with the other half.}}
# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical}} In a church: the direction of the [[altar]] and [[chancel]]; the direction faced by the priest when celebrating [[ad orientem]].
#* {{quote-book|en|date=11 April 2014|author=Paul Porwoll|title=Against All Odds: History of Saint Andrew's Parish Church, Charleston, 1706–2013|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=WestBow Press|isbn=9781490818177|pages=364–365|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=imDYAgAAQBAJ&pg=PA365
|passage=A few {{quote-gloss|Anglican churches in South Carolina, Virginia and Maryland}} are oriented other than due {{quote-gloss|geographic}} east—St. Paul's, St. George's, and Prince George's parish churches face northeast and St. Andrew's, southeast. {{...}} Throughout the book I refer directionally to the altar and chancel of St. Andrew's as situated at ''ecclesiastical'' '''east''' (to avoid overcomplicating matters), not ''geographical or magnetic'' southeast. Thus, the altar is located at the east end of the church, and the gallery, at the west.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2018|author=Anat Geva|title=Modernism and American Mid-20th Century Sacred Architecture|publisher=Routledge|isbn=9781351665339
|passage=However, in Mies&#39; chapel, liturgical '''east''' is magnetic west.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=Sarah Hosking; "Coventry Cathedral"; in Prickett Stephen Prickett|title=Edinburgh Companion to the Bible and the Arts|publisher=Edinburgh University Press|isbn=9781474471794|page=371
|passage=The tapestry by Graham Sutherland that occupies the whole wall of the liturgical '''east''' and geographic north of the cathedral is recognisable to the point of visual exhaustion.}}

