# {{lb|en|ecclesiastical}} Designating, or situated in, the liturgical east.
#: ''the '''east''' front of a cathedral''
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Paul Porwoll|title=Against All Odds: History of Saint Andrew's Parish Church, Charleston, 1706-2013|publisher=WestBow Press|isbn=9781490818177|page=365
|passage=Throughout the book I refer directionally to the altar and chancel of St. Andrew&#39;s as situated at ''ecclesiastical'' east (to avoid overcomplicating matters), not ''geographical'' or ''magnetic'' southeast. Thus, the altar is located at the '''east''' end of the church, and the gallery, at the west.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2019|author=Sarah Hosking; "Coventry Cathedral"; in Prickett Stephen Prickett|title=Edinburgh Companion to the Bible and the Arts|publisher=Edinburgh University Press|isbn=9781474471794|page=371
|passage=The tapestry by Graham Sutherland that occupies the whole wall of the liturgical '''east''' and geographic north of the cathedral is recognisable {{...}} a huge image of Christ on the [liturgical] '''east''' end<!-- [which is the geographical north end]-->, filling the entire wall and to be visible through the [liturgical] West Window (Fig. 24.2).}}

